.Dd Jun 30 2026
.Dt MQUERY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mquery
.Nd small query tool for multicast DNS
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm mquery
.Op Fl hDsTv
.Op Fl 6
.Op Fl i Ar IFACE
.Op Fl d Ar HOST
.Op Fl l Ar LEVEL
.Op Fl t Ar TYPE
.Op Fl w Ar SEC
.Op Ar NAME
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a companion tool to
.Xr mdnsd 8 ,
but can also be used stand-alone with other mDNS responders.  It sends
mDNS queries and prints the replies, much like
.Xr mdns-scan 1 ,
but it also resolves what it finds.  With
.Fl D
it follows each service PTR to its SRV, TXT, and address records and
prints a device table, and with
.Fl T
or
.Fl w
it stops on its own instead of running until interrupted.
.Pp
By default
.Nm
browses all service types on the link.  Give a service type, such as
.Cm _http._tcp ,
to list its instances, or a host or instance name together with
.Fl t
to resolve a specific record.  The
.Cm .local.\&
domain is appended automatically unless the name is already rooted with
a trailing dot.
.Pp
.Nm
runs on the system default interface, derived from the routing table;
use
.Fl i Ar IFACE
to run on another.
.Sh OPTIONS
This program follows the usual UNIX command line syntax. The options are
as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 9n
.It Fl 6
Query over IPv6 (the ff02::fb group) instead of IPv4.
.It Fl h
Print a bare bones help message and exits.
.It Fl D
Scan all services and present a deduplicated device table with name,
address, and product columns.
Use with
.Fl T
to exit automatically, or with
.Fl w Ar SEC
to set a fixed collection window.
.It Fl T
Terminate automatically after the network has been quiet for two seconds.
Intended for use with
.Fl D
or
.Fl d ,
analogous to
.Xr avahi-browse 1 Ns 's
.Fl t
option.
.It Fl d Ar HOST
Like
.Fl D ,
but filter results to
.Ar HOST
and show a detailed per-service breakdown instead of the summary table.
.Ar HOST
may be given with or without the
.Cm .local.\&
suffix.
.It Fl i Ar IFACE
Interface to run query on, by default the system default interface is
used, derived from the routing table.
.Pp
Only available on Linux.
.It Fl l Ar LEVEL
Set the log level: none, err, notice (default), info, or debug.
.It Fl s
Simple output: print one line per record as it arrives, instead of the
default browse that follows service
.Sy PTR
records.  Implied whenever
.Fl t
requests a type other than
.Sy PTR .
.It Fl t Ar TYPE
Query for the given record
.Ar TYPE ,
a numeric DNS type, default 12 (PTR).  For any type other than
.Sy PTR
the replies are printed directly, as if
.Fl s
were given.  The common types are:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "CNAME (5)XX" -compact -offset indent
.It A (1)
IPv4 address
.It NS (2)
name server
.It CNAME (5)
alias (canonical name)
.It PTR (12)
service instance or pointer
.It TXT (16)
service metadata
.It AAAA (28)
IPv6 address
.It SRV (33)
service target host and port
.It ANY (255)
any of the above
.El
.It Fl v
Show program version, along with the project homepage and the address
for bug reports.
.It Fl w Ar SEC
Delay (approximate) in seconds before automatically exiting.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Browse all service types on the link:
.Pp
.Dl mquery
.Pp
List the SSH servers, then resolve one to its host and port:
.Pp
.Dl mquery _ssh._tcp
.Dl mquery -t 33 myhost._ssh._tcp
.Pp
Look up a host's address, or a service's TXT metadata:
.Pp
.Dl mquery -t 1 myhost
.Dl mquery -t 16 myhost._ssh._tcp
.Pp
Scan the link and resolve it into a device table:
.Pp
.Dl mquery -D
.Pp
Show one device in detail, or let the scan stop itself for scripting:
.Pp
.Dl mquery -d myhost
.Dl mquery -D -T
.Pp
Query over IPv6 instead of IPv4:
.Pp
.Dl mquery -6 _http._tcp
.Pp
The
.Cm .local.\&
domain is implied in all of these, and
.Fl D
and
.Fl d
print their table on Ctrl-C or, for scripts, with
.Fl T
or
.Fl w Ar SEC .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr avahi-browse 1 ,
.Xr mdns-scan 1 ,
.Xr mdnsd 8
